<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tripartite Convention (1899)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tripartite Convention of 1899</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tripartite Convention of 1899 partitioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoan Islands</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Signed by Germany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the United States after the Second Samoan Civil War, it gave Germany the western islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savaii">Savaiʻi</a> and the United States the eastern islands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Tutuila</a>. Britain gave up its claims in return for German concessions in Tonga, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Islands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tripartite-convention-1899.jpg" alt="Tripartite Convention (1899)" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfred John Tattersall (1861-1951) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German,_British,_American_warships_in_Apia_harbour,_Samoa_1899.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the 1870s rival Samoan factions made separate treaties with the foreign powers: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> concluded a friendship treaty in 1878, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> a treaty with the same faction in 1879, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> an Anglo-Samoan treaty with a rival faction later that year. Quarrels among the foreigners and among the Samoans, sharpened by modern weapons, turned into deadly fighting. A conference in Washington in June 1887 adjourned without results, and open conflict between the powers was checked only when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> hurricane of 1889 wrecked their warships.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1889, at the invitation of the German foreign minister Count Herbert von Bismarck, the three powers set up a joint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, or condominium, with a chief justice, a municipal council for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> and a free right of the Samoans to elect their king. The arrangement soon broke down: other chiefs rebelled against the king, civil war followed, and by the end of the century the three governments freely admitted its failure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>A joint commission of the three powers arrived in Samoa in May 1899, ended the fighting and decided by July that the islands must be partitioned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, then engaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, reached an agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> signed on 14 November 1899, before Kaiser Wilhelm II's visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The convention that secured American approval was signed at Washington on 2 December 1899 by Secretary of State John Hay, Baron Theodor von Holleben and Sir Julian Pauncefote, and ratifications were exchanged on 16 February 1900. The dividing line was longitude 171 degrees west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 19 February 1900 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mckinley">William McKinley</a> placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Tutuila</a> and the islands east of the line under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Navy as a naval station; the cession of the Manua Group followed in 1904, and the name American Samoa came into use in 1905. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a>, Savaii, Apolima and Manono became German Samoa, with a flag raising on 1 March 1900 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-solf">Wilhelm Solf</a> as governor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> received the German rights in Tonga, the German islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> group east and south-east of Bougainville, a boundary in the neutral zone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and the end of German extraterritoriality in Zanzibar. These arrangements lasted until the outbreak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> in 1914.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tripartite Convention” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Tripartite Convention of 1899</b> ended the Kingdom of Samoa and gave the islands east of longitude 171 west to the United States, which prized the harbour of Pago Pago as a coaling station.</blockquote><a href="/state/american-samoa">American Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Tripartite Convention of 1899</b> partitioned the Kingdom of Samoa, Germany received the western islands of Upolu and Savaiʻi, and the colony was proclaimed at Apia on 1 March 1900.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-samoa">German Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Tripartite Convention of 1899</b> abolished the kingship and partitioned the islands into German Samoa and American Samoa.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-samoa">Kingdom of Samoa</a></li></ul></section>
